  "caption_extra": "Want the next one? DM us your topic.",
  "youtube_title": "Designed by a follower #1: fatigue fractures. Have you ever had one?",
  "why": "A stress (fatigue) fracture comes from repeated loading, not one big hit: bone constantly remodels, and when loading outpaces repair, microdamage builds into a crack (StatPearls). First described in marching soldiers in the mid-1800s as \"march foot\". The tibia is the most frequent site overall; in the foot the metatarsals (2nd and 3rd) are the most common and the calcaneus second (OrthoInfo). The navicular has a poor blood supply and a high risk of not healing; femoral neck stress fractures on the tension side carry the highest risk of breaking completely, so early detection matters (StatPearls). Risk rises with a sudden increase in activity, not eating enough for your training (REDs), and low bone strength. Pain builds with activity and eases with rest, with tenderness over one spot; early X-rays can look normal and MRI can show it. Most heal with rest and a slow, gradual return (OrthoInfo, StatPearls).",
